{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/the-crossroads-continent/1562888-at-long-last-the-return-of-the-campaign-write-up","id":1562888,"title":"At Long Last: The Return of the Campaign Write-Up","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Crossroads Continent"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-08-08T19:15:51-07:00","id":1562888,"title":"At Long Last: The Return of the Campaign Write-Up","content":"Haven't posted our gang's adventures for quite some time because the crew were exploring a ruin buried under a vegetation immersed hillock that was actually the top of an ancient temple/tomb/prison for an evil creature. And this tomb was actually an old, very old, dungeon published by TSR because it was the first tournament dungeon and I wanted our players who never experienced the classic TSR adventure modules to get an unfiltered taste of what we played back then. Playing about once a month on average, it took us this long to finish and sail north out of the swamplands in the south of the Kingdom of Twenty Horses. Since Alabaster the Winged Elf could scout above them, they found that the Hill of the Eleventh Horse is covered with ruins - and they decided to explore there.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">Climbing up to the top of the Hill of the Eleventh Horse from the moored boat down in the river, our group discovers that it is covered in overgrown ruins. The walls and such seem to indicate that the buildings were built similarly to the &quot;modern&quot; era of kings and knights like in the port cities, rather than the more ancient architecture that you've experienced in the swamp. The overgrown walls and other structures, as well as trees, make the top of the Hill a veritable maze. Alabaster flies over and finds a ruin that seems less overgrown; a large building with some smaller structures and walls still intact and visible, and the party heads that way. It is mid-morning, and the pathway becomes less overgrown with vines, grass, and shrubs, and starts to have a fair amount of various mushrooms nestled amongst the green plants. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">The area Alabaster found lies relatively open in front of an almost intact - as far you can tell under the overgrowth - temple or church and across from the large building is a roofless building that looks like a stable considering the double-wide openings that once had large doors across them, although those door rotted away long ago. There are three mounds of overgrowth equidistant around the circumfrance of the open area, and after Jiminey pulls away the vines and shrubbery, three pieces of worked stone are revealed. Trial and error suggests that the three pieces were the sides and seat of a bench.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">Brownish stains/drops are on the ground between the stables and go up the stairs and presumably into the church. Alabaster flies over the stable and lets the group know that he doesn't like what he sees, and as they enter beneath the arched doorway, they see what he means. The front end of a skeletal horse is pawing at the ground and trying to drag itself out of the building past where the group is standing. It is making but little progress, and the party can see that there are bedrolls and bedding and other signs that this building was used as a campsite for at least a couple of days. Against the shorter far wall are four large barrels.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">After some discussion, Alabaster sends forth a Thunderwave that slams everything into the back wall, shattering the horse into bone splinters.The barrels are also smashed against the wall, but hold together, with one of them leaking something that smells like beer...and from another one emanates frightened moans that are quickly stifled. After trying to communicate with whatever is within, and fruitlessly trying to remove the lid, Bruge points out that several javelins are of shoddy Wood Bugbear make - and then smashes in the lid of the barrel with his morningstar.</span></span><br /> <br /> Bruge then pulls out a whimpering Bugbear - smaller and skinnier in cruder armor - and throws him to the floor, growling &quot;Pu**y!&quot; (he's a Bugbear after all). As Bruge turns and explains to the party that the Woods Bugbears are useless, the Bugbear in question scrambles out the door and disappears behind some of the overgrown walls. The party lets him go, and searches through the debris to find a carved wooden pair of dice, and some coins. With little else of interest in the stables, they follow what is probably a blood trail back to the church stairs, and go up to the landing. There they can just make out the carved letters in Old Kingdom writing that proclaim this as a chapel for St. Denis, whom Hugh recognizes as a crusader against the undead from back when the results of the Necromancers' reign left the Kingdom teeming with undead. It's dark inside the chapel...unnaturally dark, as even the party's various darkvision senses cannot see in. Lighting a torch and trying to walk into the dark, Jiminey is disconcerted to be unable to see the torch and drops it while staggering back out to the entryway.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">Flying above, Alabaster can see that there is an almost intact tower, maybe once a bell tower, and while the chapel roof is pretty much gone, the stone framework and vaulted ceiling which was under the roof is almost intact, with the exception of a few holes. Peering through, he can see a region of darkness at the one end that coresponds to the entrance where the party waits, and then some flooring covered with detritus, the rear sections of the sidewalls and the back wall which is completely out of the darkness. After coming back and reporting, Alabaster surprises the party by shapeshifting into a bat, and then flying into the zone of darkness using echolocation to scout out the area in lieu of eyesight.\\</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">Within the darkness there are arches on either side and between them what at first appears to be a well - a walled circular area about three feet high - turns out to be the top of a spiral staircase going down. The bat, and the folks waiting outside, hear strange skittering sounds like insects clattering over the tiles, but the bat doesn't see anything moving. After the bat reports in - after shapeshifting back to Alabaster's familiar winged form - a plan is launched and executed to climb up the outer wall with Alabaster's assistance, and then use the rope to climb down at the end of the room where the darkness isn't. Once there, Asmodeus leads the way using the side wall as a guide into the darkness. Feeling his way to the archway and down the side hall, they come out of the darkness zone to find a small room that seems like the base of a tower. The remains of stone supports for stairs jut out from the inside tower wall leading up to a partial ceiling or floor. Opposite of the hall entry, where the stair would have started, is a large pile of debris completely covered over by various fungus and molds.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">At closer look the party sees a glint of metal from a ring, a ring on a skeletal hand just poking out of the debris. Before anyone can do anything, Asmodeus chops down where the wrist bones would be to separate the hand from its arm, he growls out. But there are no wrist or arm bones, just a bone hand that is joined by others - skeletal hands skittering out of the pile like twisted spiders as the Bug tries to pull off the ring - and that hand launches itself at him. Scratching, tearing, gouging, the hands are all over the dwarf as the Sea-Elf warrior attacks in a flurry of blows, smashing four of the undead creatures off the dwarf, who throws himself to the ground in an effort to crush - or at least dislodge- them! The Bug destroys the one on him with Bardic magic, and worrying that her attacks might slash the dwarf, the warrior kicks out in an attempt to smash one, but her boot lands directly in the dwarf's groin! Alabaster, who has flown high to the top of the tower, warns everyone to get out of the room, and as they rush out back into the hall right in front of the dark area - the dwarf bent over and cursing in a higher register than usual - the Winged One creates a circular Wall of Fire that immoliates the debris and rises out of the top of the top like a middle finger of flame!</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">Regrouping, they try the opposite hall only to find that once outside the zone of darkness it ends where the walls and roof collapsed. As they turn around to return, several skeletal hands drop from above, but the party destroys them and only take a little damage. They discuss heading down the central stairwell, but after they hear the now-familiar sounds of scratchy skittering from below, they opt to return to the ship and strike out again in the morning.</span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5-richt","user":"RichT","date":". 08-09-2026, 11:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5-richt","name":"RichT","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5_2.jpeg","title":"Onyx Path Creative Director","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"505"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}